WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.864 --> 00:00:04.820
- I get the question all the time,

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:06.040
wasn't it wonderful

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:09.190
in 1947 Jackie Robinson
breaks the color barrier?

00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:12.020
Opens up Major League
Baseball to everyone.

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:15.380
- My answer has to be no, it wasn't.

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:17.460
It was actually more difficult

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:19.510
for African American ballplayers.

00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:23.260
- Many talented black
players got phased out.

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:27.713
- Racism ended my professional career.

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:34.340
- The primary reason

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:37.920
for the end of the Negro
Leagues is integration.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:39.931
- What we saw with the Negro Leagues

00:00:39.931 --> 00:00:42.460
and particularly those Negro League teams

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:44.861
that were in close proximity

00:00:44.861 --> 00:00:47.800
to Major League teams they died

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:49.870
in almost instant debt

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:52.516
because that black fan base left

00:00:52.516 --> 00:00:54.200
to go see Jackie

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:56.090
to go see Larry Doby.

00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:57.580
- We're trying to get to the Major Leagues

00:00:57.580 --> 00:00:59.100
and once that was an option,

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:02.290
the segregated teams and leagues
were abandoned completely.

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:04.560
- In the fifties, the
Indianapolis clowns dropped

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:06.600
out of the Negro American league.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:08.550
- Down all the great players,

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:09.920
they've been signed to Major League.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:11.420
- Folks that own the teams

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:13.100
and that were very smart individuals,

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:14.480
they knew baseball

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:16.830
but they also knew business.

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:18.466
- Did Paula, Abe Saperstein

00:01:18.466 --> 00:01:22.490
who both had business interests
in the Harlem Globetrotters,

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:25.700
hired women to play for
the Indianapolis Clowns.

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:30.110
- Surprising that I seen women
playing in Negro Leagues.

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:31.080
They could play.

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:32.530
- Tony Stone was the first woman

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:34.620
to play for the Indianapolis Clowns.

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:37.153
- She's followed by the
Mamie Peanut Johnson

00:01:37.153 --> 00:01:38.680
and Connie Morgan

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:42.280
who competed against the men in the 1950s.

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:44.690
- Was it a promotional gimmick? Yes.

00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:46.630
Was it successful? Yes.

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:49.860
- They were trying to get
people to the ballparks.

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:51.460
And at the same time,

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:53.533
they're selling their best players

00:01:53.533 --> 00:01:55.130
to the Major Leagues,

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:57.750
trying to fill the role of
all these lost ballplayers.

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:00.115
And it really doesn't work.

00:02:00.115 --> 00:02:02.250
And if it did work for some teams,

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:05.640
it only worked maybe four, five years

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:06.810
- Unfortunately,

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:10.140
just because Jackie Robinson
integrated baseball,

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:13.395
that didn't change the fact

00:02:13.395 --> 00:02:15.360
that it was gonna be very difficult

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:16.803
for young black ballplayers.

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:20.600
- Before I leave this earth,

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:22.570
somebody out there know the answer,

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:24.950
Why is it at that time

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:28.280
they were drafting black men to go fight

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:30.510
in that foolish country,

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:32.860
but it didn't want us to play baseball

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:33.963
for this country?

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:38.320
Black men have built this country.

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:42.410
- History isn't always necessarily pretty

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:43.729
and it's not always

00:02:43.729 --> 00:02:47.260
as glamorous as people
would like it to be.

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:48.270
There were a lot of guys

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:51.310
that never got the opportunity

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:53.940
to go beyond minor league baseball.

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:56.526
- There's no question that that
was a quota system in place

00:02:56.526 --> 00:02:59.770
when that first group of
black players transition

00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:00.820
into the Major Leagues.

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:02.460
- I had the fortune of knowing a gentleman

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:04.140
by the name of Bobby Bragan.

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:06.960
Bobby was the manager for
the Pittsburgh Pirates

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:08.070
at the time.

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:09.330
He was very honest with me.

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:11.550
He said, "We had a quota
system back in those days."

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:13.310
You couldn't have more
than four black ballplayers

00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:14.170
from your team.

00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:15.620
End of story.

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:17.209
And that was pushing it.

00:03:17.209 --> 00:03:19.970
- I wanted to make it to the Major League.

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:23.970
I thought I was gonna make
it with my speed and hitting

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:25.300
because I could hit.

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:27.270
- 1950s, Jake gets signed

00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:28.960
by the Brooklyn Dodgers organization.

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:30.900
They sent him to minor
league training camp.

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:33.450
He has a phenomenal training camp.

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:36.730
The leading hitter on the
team, starting outfielder,

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:38.370
they make the cuts for the season.

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:41.130
Jake's one of the first ones to get cut.

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:44.930
Why do you cut the best
baseball player on your team?

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:47.120
- That's what hurt me so bad.

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:50.520
When they cut me for a white kid,

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:51.790
betting to sick to forward

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:53.253
and I'm betting cleanup.

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:56.320
- I was serious about the game,

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:58.130
but it was one of those things.

00:03:58.130 --> 00:04:00.160
- Pedro played everywhere

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:02.440
you can play professional baseball.

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:03.610
He played in the Negro Leagues.

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:05.349
He played in Minor League Baseball.

00:04:05.349 --> 00:04:06.960
He played in Canada.

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:08.360
He played in Dominican Republic.

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:10.180
He played in Mexico.

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:12.620
- I don't have any
regrets of what happened.

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:14.250
I thought, I think to myself

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:17.270
that I could have played.

00:04:17.270 --> 00:04:19.730
And those who played around me knew

00:04:19.730 --> 00:04:20.900
that I could.

00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:24.000
- When I got in professional baseball,

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:29.000
I didn't realize just how bad racism was.

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:30.730
- Ernest got to play

00:04:30.730 --> 00:04:33.430
in the St. Louis Cardinals organization.

00:04:33.430 --> 00:04:37.620
- The next year I was traded
to the Kansas City A's.

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:40.620
That's when racism set in.

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:42.280
My own teammate,

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:44.760
they didn't even want me to
sit on the bench with 'em.

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:46.260
Lot of people in the stand,

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:49.510
I could take that and keep walking,

00:04:49.510 --> 00:04:51.550
but my own teammate,

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:52.870
it bothered me

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:56.390
because I felt that
baseball was for everybody.

00:04:56.390 --> 00:04:58.850
They figured I'll get discouraged and quit

00:05:00.010 --> 00:05:02.930
but I told them I could play
anywhere on the baseball field.

00:05:02.930 --> 00:05:05.070
So what they started doing,

00:05:05.070 --> 00:05:07.360
they will make up excuses

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:11.410
to make sure no other team sign you.

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:14.107
They said, "You play your music too loud."

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:16.240
Man.

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:20.513
I wouldn't make a lot money
to buy stereo or radio.

00:05:22.770 --> 00:05:26.010
- If America truly appreciated
the greatest athletes,

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:28.230
there wouldn't have been a Negro League.

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:30.390
There wouldn't have been
an all white Major League.

00:05:30.390 --> 00:05:32.310
And then we would have truly seen

00:05:32.310 --> 00:05:35.020
the greatest baseball players of all time.

00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:37.110
Those aren't the truly greatest numbers

00:05:37.110 --> 00:05:39.760
because they didn't include
the truly greatest players.

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:42.600
- There has been said oh
we don't have the stats,

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:44.400
but I found score books.

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:46.520
I found in some of the black newspapers,

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:48.710
they kept score on a tally.

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:52.770
- Since 1947, think of all the records

00:05:52.770 --> 00:05:54.440
that have fallen related

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:56.532
to African American baseball players

00:05:56.532 --> 00:05:59.470
that didn't have to start in 1947.

00:05:59.470 --> 00:06:01.400
It could've started in 1907.

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:04.353
It could've started in 1887.

00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:08.630
So, based by history it would be changed,

00:06:08.630 --> 00:06:12.063
but we will never know how much.

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:16.720
- But this story,

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:19.900
the crux of it looks at a story that is

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:24.280
about the importance of
economic empowerment.

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:27.840
It is a story about an
unprecedented level of leadership

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:31.980
that emerged as a result of
the formation of these leagues.

00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:35.020
- Black cultures, black
owners, black executives,

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:37.110
they were done once the Negro Leagues died

00:06:37.110 --> 00:06:38.260
and we don't hear about them.

00:06:38.260 --> 00:06:39.670
What would it mean

00:06:39.670 --> 00:06:42.170
if black kids didn't just
wanna be the next Satchel Paige

00:06:42.170 --> 00:06:43.003
or Josh Gibson,

00:06:43.003 --> 00:06:44.900
but they wanted to be
the next Gus Greenlee

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:46.313
or the next Effa Manley.

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:51.370
- One of the things I
would like to see happen is

00:06:51.370 --> 00:06:53.880
that it would be in schools

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:58.880
because the Negro League
accomplish a lot of things

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:01.340
other than just baseball.

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:04.830
The Negro League made
not only black history,

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:06.370
American history

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:12.320
- And all of that is wrapped
around this amazing story

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:15.010
of these very courageous athletes

00:07:15.010 --> 00:07:18.080
who as I like to say
forged a glorious history

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:21.880
in the midst of an inglorious
time in American history

00:07:21.880 --> 00:07:23.230
to play the game they love.

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:26.480
- We don't want people to
forget about Josh Gibson

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:27.313
and not just Josh Gibson

00:07:27.313 --> 00:07:30.140
I'm talking about the Negro Leagues.

00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:32.640
So, what we're trying to do
is keep his legacy alive is

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:33.810
through the Josh Gibson foundation.

00:07:33.810 --> 00:07:35.980
It's making sure that
the youth is educated

00:07:35.980 --> 00:07:36.813
on the Negro Leagues

00:07:36.813 --> 00:07:39.000
as well as making sure they
get the right education

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:40.330
to further the education.

00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:42.040
So we have a Josh Gibson scholarships.

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:43.800
We have a Josh Gibson curriculum.

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:46.100
It teaches kids the
business side of sports,

00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:49.063
sports media, sports law,
sports marketing, sports sales.

00:07:53.350 --> 00:07:55.520
- When we built the Negro
League Baseball Museum

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:57.030
30 years ago,

00:07:57.030 --> 00:07:59.940
we knew then that it was
literally a race against time.

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:03.930
That those who had created this history,

00:08:03.930 --> 00:08:04.900
it wasn't a matter of if.

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:07.400
It was simply a matter of when
we were going to lose them

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:10.360
and that it was so vitally important

00:08:10.360 --> 00:08:14.620
that their story and
their legacy would play on

00:08:14.620 --> 00:08:17.680
long after there were
no Negro League players

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:21.290
to attest to the greatness
of these leagues.

00:08:21.290 --> 00:08:24.090
- A lot of the researchers
are getting older like myself.

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:25.490
You know, I'm 70 years old

00:08:25.490 --> 00:08:27.410
and it's tremendously gratifying to me

00:08:27.410 --> 00:08:31.300
that there's someone there
to carry on this legacy.

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:32.860
- As we celebrate the 100 year anniversary

00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:33.800
of the Negro Leagues,

00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:35.390
a Major League Baseball
has many commitment

00:08:35.390 --> 00:08:36.930
to also honor Negro Leagues.

00:08:36.930 --> 00:08:39.720
Back in August, they all
were to Centennial patch.

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:41.680
There's been several Major
League baseball teams

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:43.960
who recognized Negro
League baseball players.

00:08:43.960 --> 00:08:46.280
Milwaukee Brewers
celebrated Negro Leagues.

00:08:46.280 --> 00:08:48.610
Pittsburgh Pirates, Atlanta Braves,

00:08:48.610 --> 00:08:52.970
Kansas City Royals, Chicago
White Sox, Detroit Tigers

00:08:52.970 --> 00:08:55.610
by either bringing some of
these players back to honor them

00:08:55.610 --> 00:08:57.080
or just having some kind of exhibit

00:08:57.080 --> 00:08:58.553
to showcase their fans.

00:09:02.450 --> 00:09:04.600
(upbeat music)

00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:09.530
- Right now, I'm trying to
get young kids back interested

00:09:09.530 --> 00:09:10.800
in baseball.

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:12.760
I coach little league team.

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:14.893
I talk to kids.

00:09:17.630 --> 00:09:21.123
- What the Negro Leagues
teaches us is quite simple.

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:24.380
In this country of ours,

00:09:24.380 --> 00:09:26.690
if you dare to dream

00:09:26.690 --> 00:09:28.930
and you believe in yourself,

00:09:28.930 --> 00:09:32.463
you can do or be anything you want to be.

00:09:33.330 --> 00:09:34.940
- And if we were able to accomplish

00:09:34.940 --> 00:09:37.150
what we were able to accomplish in 1920

00:09:37.150 --> 00:09:39.130
with far less resources,

00:09:39.130 --> 00:09:42.090
what are we capable of now?

00:09:42.090 --> 00:09:45.720
I think the legacy honestly
is that we are more powerful

00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:47.080
than we know.

00:09:47.080 --> 00:09:49.260
- They had no idea that
they were making history.

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:51.640
They didn't care about making history.

00:09:51.640 --> 00:09:53.933
They just wanted to play ball.

00:09:55.193 --> 00:09:57.860
(upbeat music)

